From 2f6410bf37cb0633f0eb8f113f18b1202d47bd06 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 16:06:49 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] lifecycle guard: feeds through brace/if/loop/case
 groups, glued `<` on arguments, process substitution

Closes the four stdin-feed fail-opens left after PR #2316 (review 5893771725).
Each ran a lifecycle .py/.sh the guard never opened:

1. `{ cat f.py; } | python3`: at the `|` the previous segment was `}`, no cat.
   A closed group now writes on what its commands wrote (unless piped
   elsewhere inside it: `{ cat f | grep x; } | python3` stays allowed).
2. `python3 -<f.py`: an unquoted, unescaped `<`/`>` glued to a word that is
   not a bare fd number now starts its own word before shlex runs
   (`split_glued_redirections`). A quoted `<` (a quoted shell payload such
   as 'python3<f.py') stays one word and is re-scanned whole as before.
3. `cat f.py | if true; then python3; fi`: if/while/until/for/select/case
   open a group like `(`/`{`, closed by fi/done/esac in command position, so
   the pipe feeds the whole body.
4. `python3 <(cat f.py)`, `bash < <(cat f.sh)`, `source <(cat f.sh)`: a
   process substitution suspends the command it stands in; on `)` it is
   read once per file the substitution writes, in its place (a NUL
   placeholder when it writes none, so it never names a path).

The group walk moved to cron/lifecycle_guard_groups.py (lifecycle_guard.py
sits at its 2000-line cap); it takes cat_operands/opens_call_arguments/
peel_reserved_words as callables so it never imports the guard.

Tests: tests/cron/test_lifecycle_guard_compound_feeds.py. On the base
(#2316, landed as 6bc34a231f) 30 scan cases + the splitter unit test fail, covering all four
cases; `cat f.py | python3 <(echo hi)` was a base false positive (the program
is the substitution, not stdin). At head: tests/cron + the 5 other files
importing the guard, 2937 passed.
